import { JsonReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> JsonReaderCopy = JsonReaderCopy {
  summary: "Json is a JSON value whose syntax has been parsed successfully. It can represent arrays, strings, and other values as well as settings objects; it has not yet checked the fields your application needs.",
  taskTitle: "Read and rewrite JSON before choosing a settings shape",
  purpose: "Suppose you want to rewrite incoming JSON without unnecessary whitespace. Read and display both a settings object and an array, returning Invalid JSON text for malformed input. At this stage, do not extract a name or retry count.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript passes the value from JSON.parse to JSON.stringify. try/catch handles invalid syntax and returns a fallback message. Because it simply writes the JSON value back, this example does not check the fields of Settings.",
  benefit: "Seseragi Json can sit between reading text and checking application data. You can keep a successfully parsed value or write it back, then check it as Settings or extract particular fields where those are needed.",
  setup: "import * as json from \"std/json\" names the module json. fn normalize source: String -> String defines an ordinary function taking a string named source and returning a string. The argument type follows the colon; the return type follows ->.",
  after: [
    "json.parse source reads source as JSON. Function arguments follow the function name, separated by spaces. Its result is Either<json.JsonParseError, json.Json>: Left carries a parsing failure, and Right carries the parsed Json. Either is the type holding one of these two outcomes.",
    "match examines that result. Right value -> names the parsed Json value and writes it back with json.stringify value. Left _ -> selects the failure message; _ means the detailed error is unused. Each -> leads to the expression for that branch.",
    "The first input loses its outer whitespace; the second loses unnecessary whitespace inside the array. true, null, and the string ready remain JSON values. The final { has invalid syntax, so it produces Invalid JSON text. In the source, \\\" writes a double quote inside a string.",
    "pub effect fn main is the output entry point; Unit means no useful return value. with Console fails ConsoleError declares console use and possible output failure. do runs println calls in order, and _ <- discards the earlier print results. The parentheses in println (normalize ...) group the inner call so its resulting string can be displayed.",
  ],
  details: [
    "Json has six forms: JsonNull for null, JsonBool for a boolean, JsonNumber for a number, JsonString for text, JsonArray for an array of Json, and JsonObject for named Json values. These public names identify the forms of a value; match can branch on them when needed. Successful parsing alone does not even guarantee an object.",
    "JsonNumber keeps numbers as Decimal rather than rounding them into JavaScript number values. JsonObject stores fields in an ordered Map. It is not a JavaScript object passed through unchanged. Large numbers and integer-looking field names can behave differently from native JSON.parse.",
    "Reading rejects duplicate field names. Writing does not preserve whitespace or the original number spelling; it produces a compact representation of the JSON value. It is not a way to reproduce a document's original appearance. Matching output for these examples does not establish identical JavaScript behavior for all inputs.",
    "To use name in an application, continue with field and a String checker. To use the complete Settings, continue with decodeJson and checking support for Settings. If you want to read text directly as Settings, decodeString combines those two stages.",
  ],
}
